    --secondary: {C_SECONDARY};
    --accent: {C_ACCENT};
    --fiction: {C_FICTION};
    --nonfiction: {C_NONFICTION};
    --text: {C_TEXT};
    --muted: {C_MUTED};
    --bg-light: {C_BG_LIGHT};
    --border: {C_BORDER};
    --success: {C_SUCCESS};
    --warning: {C_WARNING};
    --danger: {C_DANGER};
  }}
  * {{ margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }}
  body {{
    font-family: 'Segoe UI', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;
    color: var(--text);
    background: #f0f4f8;
    line-height: 1.65;
    font-size: 15px;
  }}
  .container {{ max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px; }}

  /* ── Header ── */
  .report-header {{
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--primary) 0%, #2d5a8e 100%);
    color: white;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }}
  .report-header h1 {{
    font-size: 28px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 8px; letter-spacing: -0.3px;
  }}
  .report-header .subtitle {{
    font-size: 16px; opacity: 0.85; margin-bottom: 16px;
  }}
  .report-header .meta {{
    font-size: 13px; opacity: 0.7;
  }}

  /* ── Sections ── */
  .section {{
    background: white;
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 36px 40px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }}
  .section h2 {{
    font-size: 22px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--primary);
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--secondary);
    padding-bottom: 10px; margin-bottom: 20px;
  }}
  .section h3 {{
    font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--primary);
    margin: 24px 0 12px;
  }}
  .section p {{
    margin-bottom: 14px; color: var(--text);
  }}

  /* ── KPI Cards ── */
  .kpi-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 20px 0 28px;
  }}
  .kpi-card {{
    background: var(--bg-light);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--secondary);
    text-align: center;
  }}
  .kpi-card .kpi-value {{
    font-size: 26px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--primary);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }}
  .kpi-card .kpi-label {{
    font-size: 12px; color: var(--muted); text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px; font-weight: 600;
  }}

  /* ── Charts ── */
  .chart-container {{
    text-align: center;
    margin: 24px 0;
  }}
  .chart-container img {{
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 6px;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);
  }}

  /* ── Tables ── */
  .data-table {{
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 20px 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }}
  .data-table th {{
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.3px;
  }}
  .data-table td {{
    padding: 9px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }}
  .data-table tr:nth-child(even) {{ background: #f8fafc; }}
  .data-table tr:hover {{ background: #edf2f7; }}

  /* ── Callouts ── */
  .callout {{
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    margin: 16px 0;
    border-left: 4px solid;
  }}
  .callout-insight {{
    background: #ebf8ff; border-color: var(--secondary);
  }}
  .callout-warning {{
    background: #fefcbf; border-color: var(--warning);
  }}
  .callout-success {{
    background: #f0fff4; border-color: var(--success);
  }}
  .callout strong {{ display: block; margin-bottom: 4px; }}

  /* ── Comparison Table ── */
  .compare-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }}
  .compare-col {{
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px 24px;
  }}
  .compare-col.fiction {{
    background: #ebf4ff;
    border: 2px solid var(--fiction);
  }}
  .compare-col.nonfiction {{
    background: #fff5eb;
    border: 2px solid var(--nonfiction);
  }}
  .compare-col h4 {{
    font-size: 16px; margin-bottom: 12px;
  }}
  .compare-col.fiction h4 {{ color: var(--fiction); }}
  .compare-col.nonfiction h4 {{ color: var(--nonfiction); }}
  .compare-metric {{
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 6px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
    font-size: 14px;
  }}
  .compare-metric:last-child {{ border-bottom: none; }}
  .compare-metric .value {{ font-weight: 700; }}

  /* ── Recommendations ── */
  .rec-card {{
    background: var(--bg-light);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 18px 22px;
    margin: 14px 0;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
  }}
  .rec-card .rec-title {{
    font-weight: 700; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 15px;
  }}
  .rec-card .rec-evidence {{
    font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); font-style: italic;
  }}

  /* ── Footer ── */
  .report-footer {{
    text-align: center;
    padding: 32px 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 12.5px;
  }}

  /* ── Badge ── */
  .badge {{
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 2px 10px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }}
  .badge-fic {{ background: #dbeafe; color: var(--fiction); }}
  .badge-nf {{ background: #ffedd5; color: var(--nonfiction); }}

  /* ── Responsive ── */
  @media (max-width: 768px) {{
    .section {{ padding: 24px 20px; }}
    .compare-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
    .kpi-grid {{ grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }}
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ━━━ HEADER ━━━ -->
<div class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis (2009–2019)</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Source: Amazon Top 50 Bestsellers | 550 Entries Across 11 Years | Report Date: May 2026</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ━━━ SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW ━━━ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>1. Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
  <p>
    This analysis examines 550 bestseller entries from Amazon's annual Top 50 lists spanning 2009 to 2019.
    Because the same title can appear in multiple years, the dataset captures both perennial favorites and
    one-year phenomena, providing a rich view of pricing and satisfaction dynamics over a full decade.
  </p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{total_records}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Bestseller Entries</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{unique_books}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Titles</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{unique_authors}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Authors</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{avg_rating:.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Average Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">${avg_price:.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Average Price</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{avg_reviews:,.0f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Reviews</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_genre_pie}" alt="Genre Distribution">
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-insight">
    <strong>Key Takeaway</strong>
    Non-Fiction titles account for {nf_pct:.1f}% of all bestseller entries ({nf_count} entries) versus Fiction at {fic_pct:.1f}% ({fic_count} entries).
    Non-Fiction's dominance reflects sustained demand for self-help, cookbooks, and reference titles that
    maintain bestseller status across multiple years.
  </div>

  <h3>Price and Rating Landscape</h3>
  <p>
    Bestseller prices range from <strong>${min_price}</strong> (free promotional/kindle titles) to <strong>${max_price}</strong> (specialty reference like the DSM-5),
    with a median of <strong>${median_price:.2f}</strong>. Ratings cluster tightly between <strong>{min_rating:.1f}</strong> and <strong>{max_rating:.1f}</strong>,
    confirming that bestseller status self-selects for well-received books — the real variation is in the
    sub-4.5 vs. 4.7+ band.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- ━━━ SECTION 2: PRICE-RATING CORRELATION ━━━ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>2. Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
  <p>
    The central question: does paying more for a bestselling book correlate with higher customer satisfaction?
    To answer this, we compute the <strong>Pearson correlation coefficient</strong> between Price and User Rating
    across the full dataset, and separately by genre.
  </p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--primary);">
      <div class="kpi-value">{pearson_r:.3f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall Pearson r</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--fiction);">
      <div class="kpi-value">{fic_r:.3f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Fiction Pearson r</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--nonfiction);">
      <div class="kpi-value">{nf_r:.3f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Non-Fiction Pearson r</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--muted);">
      <div class="kpi-value">{pearson_p:.4f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall p-value</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_scatter}" alt="Price vs Rating Scatter">
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <strong>Statistically Significant but Weak Negative Correlation</strong>
    The overall Pearson r of <strong>{pearson_r:.3f}</strong> (p = {pearson_p:.4f}) indicates a statistically significant
    but weak negative relationship between price and user rating. In practical terms, higher-priced bestsellers
    tend to receive marginally <em>lower</em> ratings — not higher. The effect is small (r² ≈ {pearson_r**2:.3f}),
    meaning price explains only about {pearson_r**2*100:.1f}% of the variance in ratings.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_scatter_genre}" alt="Price vs Rating by Genre">
  </div>

  <h3>Genre-Specific Correlation</h3>
  <p>
    The negative correlation holds in both genres: Fiction (r = {fic_r:.3f}, p = {fic_p:.4f}) and
    Non-Fiction (r = {nf_r:.3f}, p = {nf_p:.4f}). Non-Fiction shows a slightly stronger negative relationship,
    likely because expensive reference titles (e.g., DSM-5 at $105, APA Publication Manual at $46) tend to
    receive more utilitarian — and therefore more moderate — ratings compared to beloved fiction at lower price points.
  </p>

  <h3>Price Segment Analysis</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_price_segments}" alt="Price Segment Distribution">
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Price Segment</th>
        <th>Entries</th>
        <th>Share</th>
        <th>Avg Rating</th>
        <th>Avg Reviews</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {segment_table_rows}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-insight">
    <strong>Low-Priced Books Dominate the Bestseller List</strong>
    Budget and Mid-Range titles ($1–$15) account for the majority of bestseller entries, and they also
    carry the highest average ratings. Free titles ($0) appear sporadically and tend to earn strong ratings,
    while High-End titles ($26+) are rare and show slightly lower average satisfaction.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ━━━ SECTION 3: GENRE COMPARISON ━━━ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>3. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>
  <p>
    A side-by-side comparison of the two genres reveals systematic differences in pricing, customer
    satisfaction, and engagement patterns that have direct implications for catalog and pricing strategy.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_genre_compare}" alt="Genre Comparison">
  </div>

  <div class="compare-grid">
    <div class="compare-col fiction">
      <h4><span class="badge badge-fic">Fiction</span></h4>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Bestseller Entries</span><span class="value">{fic_count}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Average Price</span><span class="value">${fic_avg_price:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Median Price</span><span class="value">${fic_median_price:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Average Rating</span><span class="value">{fic_avg_rating:.3f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Average Reviews</span><span class="value">{fic_avg_reviews:,.0f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Price-Rating r</span><span class="value">{fic_r:.3f}</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-col nonfiction">
      <h4><span class="badge badge-nf">Non-Fiction</span></h4>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Bestseller Entries</span><span class="value">{nf_count}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Average Price</span><span class="value">${nf_avg_price:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Median Price</span><span class="value">${nf_median_price:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Average Rating</span><span class="value">{nf_avg_rating:.3f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Average Reviews</span><span class="value">{nf_avg_reviews:,.0f}</span></div>
      <div class="compare-metric"><span>Price-Rating r</span><span class="value">{nf_r:.3f}</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-insight">
    <strong>Genre Dynamics at a Glance</strong>
    {"Fiction bestsellers are priced lower on average but earn slightly higher ratings and significantly more reviews — suggesting stronger reader engagement and perceived value." if fic_avg_price < nf_avg_price and fic_avg_rating > nf_avg_rating else "Genre dynamics show distinct patterns in pricing and satisfaction."}
    Non-Fiction commands a higher average price (${nf_avg_price:.2f} vs. ${fic_avg_price:.2f}),
    {"but earns a marginally lower average rating" if nf_avg_rating < fic_avg_rating else "and earns a comparable rating"}
    ({nf_avg_rating:.3f} vs. {fic_avg_rating:.3f}).
    {"Fiction generates substantially more reviews per title" if fic_avg_reviews > nf_avg_reviews else "Review volumes are comparable between genres"}
    ({fic_avg_reviews:,.0f} vs. {nf_avg_reviews:,.0f}), indicating higher reader engagement in the Fiction segment.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ━━━ SECTION 4: YEAR-OVER-YEAR TRENDS ━━━ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
  <p>
    This section tracks how pricing and satisfaction evolved annually from 2009 to 2019. Inflection points
    — years where pricing strategy or customer sentiment visibly shifted — are highlighted to identify
    structural changes in the bestseller market.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_yoy_price}" alt="Average Price by Year">
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_yoy_rating}" alt="Average Rating by Year">
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_yoy_reviews}" alt="Average Reviews by Year">
  </div>

  <h3>Dual-Axis View: Price and Rating Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_dual_axis}" alt="Dual Axis Price and Rating">
  </div>

  <h3>Annual Breakdown with Year-over-Year Change</h3>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Year</th>
        <th>Avg Price</th>
        <th>Price YoY</th>
        <th>Avg Rating</th>
        <th>Rating YoY</th>
        <th>Avg Reviews</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {yoy_table_rows}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Inflection Points</h3>
  <p>
    The data reveals several notable shifts over the decade:
  </p>
  <ul style="margin-left:20px; margin-bottom:16px;">
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
      <strong>2009–2011 — High-price plateau:</strong> Average prices held above $14, driven by hardcover
      bestsellers and specialty reference titles. This was the pre-e-book-disruption era.
    </li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
      <strong>2012–2014 — Price compression begins:</strong> Average prices declined as digital formats and
      competitive pricing put downward pressure on bestseller list prices. The shift coincides with the
      maturation of Kindle and e-reader markets.
    </li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
      <strong>2015 — Price trough:</strong> Average price hit its lowest point in the decade at
      ${yearly.loc[yearly['avg_price'].idxmin(), 'avg_price']:.2f}, reflecting the proliferation of
      low-priced coloring books and children's titles on the bestseller list.
    </li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
      <strong>2016–2019 — Gradual price recovery:</strong> Prices recovered modestly as illustrated editions,
      boxed sets, and premium-priced non-fiction re-entered the bestseller mix.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-insight">
    <strong>Ratings Defy Price Trends</strong>
    While average prices fluctuated significantly over the decade, user ratings showed a remarkably
    stable upward trend — from {yearly.iloc[0]['avg_rating']:.3f} in {int(yearly.iloc[0]['Year'])}
    to {yearly.iloc[-1]['avg_rating']:.3f} in {int(yearly.iloc[-1]['Year'])}.
    This divergence confirms that rating quality on the bestseller list is driven by content merit,
    not price positioning. Customers rate the reading experience, not the price tag.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-success">
    <strong>Review Volume Surge</strong>
    Average review counts increased dramatically across the decade — from {yearly.iloc[0]['avg_reviews']:,.0f}
    in {int(yearly.iloc[0]['Year'])} to {yearly.iloc[-1]['avg_reviews']:,.0f} in {int(yearly.iloc[-1]['Year'])}.
    This reflects Amazon's growing user base, increased review-culture participation, and the compounding
    effect of perennial bestsellers accumulating reviews over time.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ━━━ SECTION 5: TOP AUTHORS ━━━ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>5. Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</h2>
  <p>
    Sustained bestseller presence requires both prolific output and consistent reader approval.
    The following analysis identifies the authors who appear most frequently across the decade and
    examines how their pricing and ratings compare.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_top_authors}" alt="Top Authors">
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>#</th>
        <th>Author</th>
        <th>Appearances</th>
        <th>Unique Titles</th>
        <th>Avg Rating</th>
        <th>Avg Price</th>
        <th>Avg Reviews</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {author_table_rows}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-insight">
    <strong>Perennial Dominance Driven by Series and Reference</strong>
    {"Jeff Kinney" if author_freq.iloc[0]['Author'] == 'Jeff Kinney' else author_freq.iloc[0]['Author']}
    leads with {int(author_freq.iloc[0]['appearances'])} appearances — a product of the
    Diary of a Wimpy Kid franchise consistently landing on annual bestseller lists.
    {"American Psychological Association" if 'American Psychological Association' in author_freq['Author'].values else "Reference publishers"}
    appear frequently through a single perennial title (APA Publication Manual), demonstrating that
    institutional/reference titles can sustain bestseller status indefinitely regardless of price point.
    Most top authors price in the mid-range and earn ratings above 4.5 — consistent with the broader
    finding that moderate pricing aligns with strong satisfaction.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ━━━ SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS ━━━ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p>
    The following recommendations are derived directly from the data findings above. Each recommendation
    traces back to a specific statistical observation — no speculative advice is included.
  </p>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">1. Target the $5–$15 Price Band for New Bestseller-List Titles</div>
    <p>
      Budget and Mid-Range price segments dominate the bestseller list and carry the highest average
      ratings. New titles aiming for bestseller-list placement should be priced competitively in this
      range rather than at premium tiers.
    </p>
    <div class="rec-evidence">
      Evidence: Mid-Range ($9–$15) segment has the highest entry count with strong ratings;
      Pearson r = {pearson_r:.3f} shows no rating premium for higher prices.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">2. Avoid Overpricing Non-Fiction Reference Titles</div>
    <p>
      High-priced Non-Fiction titles ($25+) show the weakest ratings in the dataset. While specialty
      reference works (like the DSM-5) will always have a market, their lower ratings suggest that
      pricing above $25 creates an expectation gap that customers penalize in reviews.
    </p>
    <div class="rec-evidence">
      Evidence: Non-Fiction Pearson r = {nf_r:.3f} (stronger negative correlation than Fiction's {fic_r:.3f});
      High-End segment avg rating is the lowest across all segments.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">3. Invest in Fiction for Higher Engagement and Organic Reviews</div>
    <p>
      Fiction generates {abs(fic_avg_reviews - nf_avg_reviews):,.0f} more average reviews per title
      than Non-Fiction. Higher review volumes amplify visibility on Amazon's recommendation algorithms
      and create a virtuous cycle of discovery and sales.
    </p>
    <div class="rec-evidence">
      Evidence: Fiction avg reviews = {fic_avg_reviews:,.0f} vs. Non-Fiction = {nf_avg_reviews:,.0f};
      Fiction avg rating = {fic_avg_rating:.3f} vs. Non-Fiction = {nf_avg_rating:.3f}.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">4. Leverage Perennial Titles and Franchise Strategy</div>
    <p>
      Authors with the most bestseller appearances (e.g., Jeff Kinney, Dr. Seuss, Gary Chapman)
      succeed through repeated releases in a franchise or series format. Publishers should invest in
      series development over one-off titles to maximize list longevity.
    </p>
    <div class="rec-evidence">
      Evidence: Top 10 authors average {author_freq.head(10)['appearances'].mean():.1f} appearances
      across the decade; series authors (Kinney, Riordan, Rowling) dominate the frequency chart.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">5. Price Aggressively During Market Compression Periods</div>
    <p>
      The data shows that average bestseller prices compress during market disruptions (2012–2015) and
      recover slowly. During similar future disruptions, pricing below the market average can capture
      bestseller-list share while ratings remain unaffected — customers do not rate based on price.
